{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"2b4333f04533c137","type":"text","text":"Fast and Efficient Hardware Implementation of HQC\nhttps://eprint.iacr.org/2022/1183","x":-200,"y":-300,"width":585,"height":100},
		{"id":"20994a817c426765","type":"text","text":"Algorithm 2 (HQC.PKE.KeyGen() and HQC.KEM.KeyGen())\n- Hamming weights $w$ are hardcoded depending on security level\n1) $h \\leftarrow \\textit{R}$\n\t- A polynomial $h$ is chosen uniformly randomly from the quotient ring $\\textit{R}$ on which vectors and operations of HQC are defined\n\t- So, a random binary vector of length $n$\n2) $(x,y) \\leftarrow^w \\textit{R}^2$\n\t- Polynomials $(x,y)$ are chosen uniformly random from $R^2$ (I believe a cartesian product) with a Hamming weight of $w$ (Choose $w$ positions to be 1, rest 0)\n\t- Something about sparsity here and how it's analogous to LWE's short vectors (?)\n\t- $(x,y)$ are the secret vectors of HQC\n3) $s := x+h\\cdot y$ \n\t- Let s be $x+h\\cdot y$\n\t- Since h and y are binary vectors, product is cyclical convolution with coefficients mod 2\n\t\t- $h$ is the random matrix\n\t\t- $y$ is the secret key\n\t\t- $x$ is the error term\n4) **return** $(pk := (h,s), sk := (x,y))$\n\t- Public key is the pair, random polynomial $h$ and the computed polynomial $s$\n\t- Secret key is the pair of sparse polynomials $(x,y)$","x":-200,"y":-140,"width":585,"height":820},
		{"id":"c988c97f555a4499","type":"text","text":"Algorithm 3 HQC.PKE.Encrypt(pk = (h, s), m, $\\theta$)\n- Provided the public key (containing random polynomial $h$ and polynomial compute $s$), the message $m$, and a noise generated seed, $\\theta$\n- Hamming weights $w_r$ are hardcoded depending on security level\n\t- Not really sure where $\\theta$ comes from yet - PRG maybe?\n1) $(r_1, r_2, e) \\leftarrow^{w_r, \\theta} \\textit{R}^3$ \n\t- Masking noise polynomials $r_1, r_2$ are sampled uniformly random from $\\textit{R}^3$ with Hamming weight $w_r$\n\t- Message noise polynomial $e$ is sampled uniformly random from $R^3$ with Hamming weight $\\theta$\n2) $u := r_1 + h \\cdot r_2$\n\t- Let $u$ be $r_1 + h \\cdot r_2$\n\t- Same as $s$ compute in KeyGen algorithm\n\t- Encodes $h$ with noisy polynomials $r_1, r_2$\n3) $t := Encode(m)$\n\t- Let $t$ be the encoded message $m$\n\t- Encoding is done by matrix vector multiplication between a hardcoded Generator matrix and a message - encode function of concatenated Reed-Muller and Reed-Solon codes\n\t\t- Stated in Definition 3.4.1 in HQC specifications:\n\t\t  \n\t- The paper noted that given $c = mG$, the codeword is given by duplicating $c$ 3 or 5 times depending on security level\n4) $v := t + s \\cdot r_2 + e$\n\t- Let $v$ be the compute of the masked message (hides encoded message using public key with some noise $e$)\n5) **return** $c := (u,v)$\n\t- Returns the ciphertext comprised of compute polynomials $u,v$","x":460,"y":-140,"width":585,"height":820},
		{"id":"60c2fef383b74e2b","type":"text","text":"Algorithm 4 HQC.PKE.Decrypt(sk = (x, y), c = (u, v))\n- Provided the two generated key's secret key $x,y$ and the ciphertext $u,v$ \n1) $m' := Decode(v - u \\cdot y)$\n\t- Let $m'$ be the decoded message $m$\n\t- Decoding is done by matrix vector multiplication between a hardcoded Generator matrix and a message - encode function of concatenated Reed-Muller and Reed-Solon codes\n\t- The paper noted that given $c = mG$, the codeword is given by duplicating $c$ 3 or 5 times depending on security level\n\t- Noted in the specifications: \n\t\t- Decoder is an algebraic decoder for the Reed-Solomon code\n2) **return** $m'$","x":1120,"y":-140,"width":585,"height":820},
		{"id":"5eebb1b7627d85d0","x":460,"y":1115,"width":585,"height":880,"type":"text","text":"RS Encoding;\nPer the HQC specifications\n- Encode function takes K-bit input and first encodes it with the Reed-Solomon code\n- Let $p$ be a prime number and $q$ is any power of $p$ \n\t- Block length $n=q-1$\n\t- Min distance $d_{min}=2\\delta +1$\n\t\t- $2\\delta = n-k$ with $\\delta$ as the correcting cap of the code and $k$ as the number of info bits\n\t- $\\alpha$ is a primitive element in the binary field - the generator polynomial $g(x) = (x+\\alpha)(x+\\alpha^2)...(x+\\alpha^{2\\delta})$ \n- For HQC, the binary field $\\mathbb{F}_{2^m}$ where $m=8$ \n\t- Thus primitive polynomial $g(x) = 1+\\alpha^2+\\alpha^3+\\alpha^4+\\alpha^8$\n\nThe encoding process can be summarized as such:\nEncoding is was summarized as the following systemic flow:\n1) Multiply the message u(x) by $x^{n-k}$\n\t- $x^{n-k} \\Rightarrow$ Shift message left by $n-k$ positions \n2) Compute the remainder b(x) by dividing $x^{n-k}u(x)$ by gen poly g(x)\n\t- $b(x) = x^{n-k}u(x)\\: \\text{mod}\\: g(x)$ \n\t- So, $b(x)$ is the parity shift\n3) Combine b(x) and x^{x-k}u(x) to obtain the code polynomial $c(x) = b(x) + x^{n-k}u(x)$\n\nRM Encoding:\nPer the HQC specifications\n- HQC uses duplicate RM codes, where using first order RM (denoted RM(1,7)) "},
		{"id":"98313aa33eefbe61","x":460,"y":760,"width":585,"height":275,"type":"text","text":"Deshpande et al:\n- Achieves step (1) by passing $m_{in}$ of length $K$  into a shift register\n- Achieves step (2) by passing Shifted $m_{in}$ into Galois Field multipliers\n\t- Galois multipliers were said to have been designed via an LFSR-based optimization multiplication unit \n\t- Galois multipliers are only used in Solomon encoding\n- Achieves step (3) by passing Galois mult outputs into LSFR after each cycle"},
		{"id":"80d9aee55855941a","x":1120,"y":1295,"width":585,"height":520,"type":"text","text":"Per this article: https://tomverbeure.github.io/2022/08/07/Reed-Solomon.html (Reed-Solomon encoding)\nExample;\n- Given:\n\t- Alphabets are ints\n\t- Size $k$ of message $m$ is $4$\n\t- Want $2$ redundant symbols so code word has length $n=6$ \n\t\t- Since $6-4=2$ \n\t- Polynomial $p(x)$ is always evaluated for the following values of $x: (-1, 0, 1, 2, 3, 4)$ . Note there are $x_i$ values as there are symbols in the code word\n- Encoding:\n\t- Create polynomial $p(x)=2+3x-5x^2+x^3$ (Uses message word symbols as coefficients)\n\t- Evaluate $p(x)$ at the 6 locations of $x$\n\t\t- $(−1,7),(0,2),(1,1),(2,−4),(3,−7),(4,−2)$\n\t\t- Thus the code word is $(7,2,1,−4,−7,−2)$"}
	],
